1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,428
By March the map had grown into an argument.

2
00:00:02,428 --> 00:00:10,963
A college leader wanted to hand it to the university administration; a student group wanted to keep it personal.

3
00:00:10,963 --> 00:00:27,648
“A president signs a policy,” Maya said, “but a student stands in the rain.” She meant that two priorities could not both come first, and that a student with wet hair in a thin coat had no vote in either plan.

4
00:00:27,648 --> 00:00:39,149
The disagreement was not about ability; it was about who owned the story, and individualism was not the enemy of a shared map — silence was.

5
00:00:39,149 --> 00:00:42,654
On campus the argument had a history.

6
00:00:42,654 --> 00:01:00,644
A senior dean with a grey beard blew a small horn for tradition, and a young lecturer brushed the dust off an old statistic: the rise of youth enrolment had changed the undergraduate body, and each dormitory now held a different idea of adulthood.

7
00:01:00,644 --> 00:01:07,039
A young woman in the dormitory laughed at the horn, and Maya wrote her words down.

8
00:01:07,039 --> 00:01:12,704
Neither a gown nor a lecture could settle the question of identity.

9
00:01:12,704 --> 00:01:18,489
Lin's major was environmental engineering, and he trusted numbers.

10
00:01:18,489 --> 00:01:23,590
Maya studied journalism, and she trusted persons.

11
00:01:23,590 --> 00:01:28,140
“An individual is not a statistic,” she said.

12
00:01:28,140 --> 00:01:37,300
Professor Zhao called this the dense nucleus of the problem: growth has an extension, a personality, and a cost.

13
00:01:37,300 --> 00:01:47,670
In the spring the campus turned green, and a poster about nutrition and protein looked more urgent than a lecture on cultivation.

14
00:01:47,670 --> 00:02:03,491
“You promote the map,” Maya said, “and they promote themselves.” A rag of an old notice, mold on the wall behind it, and a letter from an academician were the only evidence the office had produced.

15
00:02:03,491 --> 00:02:10,096
There was no promotion and no budget line, only a rush to finish before the summer.

16
00:02:10,096 --> 00:02:23,006
Maya's grandmother had died of cancer the year before; she did not say it to win the argument, but the room went quiet when she mentioned the bus that took two hours.

17
00:02:23,006 --> 00:02:27,916
The second opportunity came from the alumni office itself.

18
00:02:27,916 --> 00:02:35,101
Among the three hundred replies, a hundred had come from people who could not afford a taxi.

19
00:02:35,101 --> 00:02:40,736
“Why do those numbers matter?” a gentleman from that office asked.

20
00:02:40,736 --> 00:02:47,976
“Because a lady in the dorm and a girl in the laboratory rely on the same bus,” Maya said.

21
00:02:47,976 --> 00:02:57,341
The group agreed to develop the map in two versions, to alter nothing without asking, and also to publish its cost.

22
00:02:57,341 --> 00:03:05,152
Confidence, Lin wrote, is not faith; it is the amount of evidence you are willing to show.

23
00:03:05,152 --> 00:03:13,442
Professor Zhao encouraged them, and the answer to the disagreement was yes and no at once.
